WRITTEN ARGUMENTS ON BEHALF OF THE ACCUSED

RESPECTFULLY SUBMITTED:

I. CONSENSUAL RELATIONSHIP

The applicant and the victim were in a consensual relationship for over six months. This is evident from the chat messages and call records between them. The relationship was acknowledged by both parties.

II. DISPUTE OVER MARRIAGE PROMISE

The allegation arises from a dispute over marriage promise. The victim was upset when the applicant expressed inability to marry immediately. This is a civil dispute, not a criminal offence.

III. DELAY IN FIR

The FIR was filed after a delay of 3 days. No satisfactory explanation has been provided for this delay. The delay indicates that the allegation is an afterthought and not genuine.

IV. MEDICAL EVIDENCE

The medical examination report does not support the allegation of rape. There are no injuries indicative of force. The medical findings are consistent with consensual sexual activity.

V. ABSENCE OF FORCE

There is no evidence of force, threat, or intimidation. The victim never raised any alarm during the alleged incident. There were no witnesses who heard any commotion.

VI. PRIOR CONDUCT

The applicant has no criminal antecedents. He is a law-abiding citizen. The complaint is motivated by anger over the marriage dispute.

VII. CREDIBILITY OF COMPLAINT

The complaint is not supported by independent evidence. The version given by the victim is inconsistent with the available evidence. The complaint appears to be motivated by revenge.

VIII. LEGAL POSITION

The Supreme Court has held that in cases arising from marriage promise disputes, the court must examine whether there was a false promise from the beginning or a genuine promise that could not be fulfilled. In the present case, the promise was genuine but circumstances changed.

PRAYER

It is therefore prayed that the applicant be granted bail and the charge of rape be not framed as the ingredients of Section 376 BNS are not made out.

